[StudyName "The Fried Liver Attack!"]
[ChapterName "Italian Game: Knight Attack - Black plays the Polerio Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/v9TezkMy/GLPYVloF"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/thereal_stockfish"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3Pp1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.07.07"]
[UTCTime "01:18:50"]
[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

6... Na5! { The top engine move after White takes on d5 is not taking back with the knight. It's the move Na5, attacking the bishop! This is the best defensive plan for Black to stop the Fried Liver. Black can always take our bishop and the knight on f6 is still... on f6.
To get out of this, let's move the bishop to safety while giving a check at the same time. } { [%cal Gc4b5,Ra5c4] } 7. Bb5+! { Good job! Black now has to deal with that annoying check. c6 is almost always played, with a play rate of over 80%. } { [%cal Rc7c6,Gb5e8] } 7... c6 { In most games, black plays c6, blocking the check. We can just take the pawn with dxc6. } 8. dxc6 { In an overwhelming 94% of times, Black takes with the pawn. In the other 6% of cases, Black takes with the knight. I'll only focus on bxc6 today, though. } { [%cal Rb7c6,Ra5c6] } 8... bxc6 { Here we play Bd3, retreating from danger.
According to the lichess database, White has a 52% win rate after playing Bd3.
Stockfish says the position is equal, but Black has an isolated pawn, a weird 'knight on the rim', and we're just up a pawn. We can then start development while Black's queenside is open and the pawns are scattered.
Another interesting move here is Qf3, the Bogoljubov variation. Sometimes you can catch an opponent with it. They'll get the bishop, but lose the rook. } { [%csl Rc6,Gb5][%cal Rc6b5,Gb5d3,Gf3a8,Gd1f3] } *

