[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.07.07"]
[UTCTime "01:13:29"]
[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

8... Ke8 { Let's look at Black's second most common response to the Fried Liver attack, Ke8. It turns out, this move basically loses on the spot.
That's a free knight right there, and after that, we're not only up a pawn, we're threatening checkmate with Qf7#. Let's take that knight! } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Gc4d5,Gd5f7,Gf3f7,Gf7e8,Gf7d7] } 9. Bxd5 { Great! Now black has to deal with the checkmate threat and they just lost their knight! White is completely winning!
Stockfish says this is +2.1 evaluation for White. Lichess's database gives this an 82% win rate for white.
Incredibly, 45% of players don't even prevent the checkmate! } { [%csl Re8][%cal Gd5f7,Gf3f7,Gf7e8,Gf7d7] } 9... Qe7 { If your opponent plays this move to stop checkmate, you can get away with a sneaky trap!
Take that knight with your bishop! } 10. Bxc6+ { Some people playing as Black look into the future and block the check with the bishop, Some don't.
83% of people don't. } { [%cal Rc8d7,Gc6e8] } 10... bxc6?? { Now Black's dead lost. Taking that pawn is a clean fork and you win the rook no matter what. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Gf3c6,Gc6e8,Gc6a8] } 11. Qxc6+ Kf7 12. Qxa8 { Look at that. You can probably also pick some pawns up on your way out.
Black's position is completely ravaged, and you're ready to castle. Stockfish evaluates this at +6.1, and you have an 86% win rate as White! } { [%cal Ga8a7,Ga8c8,Re7e8] } *

